{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfSR39vqnpXEgHuy2c25o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ctx5YLJfX9yL6b2WGfJsof","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"<redacted> encounter details: travel time, closest approach date/time/range, Jan 1986 start of observations, signal time, light levels, moons discovered and names, rings, magnetic field tilt, wind speeds, Miranda range, Challenger date, Saturn geometry days."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12257,"cache_read_input_tokens":1385,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12257},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7f4113a-c749-4d8d-8d8d-18d3200e9258","uuid":"1713c31a-f6a9-4347-a5e6-02ca609c2ac9","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:05:33.312Z","request_id":"req_011CfSR39YHKWyEh2qYH7U9i","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Ctx5YLJfX9yL6b2WGfJsof":{"url":"<redacted> encounter details: travel time, closest approach date/time/range, Jan 1986 start of observations, signal time, light levels, moons discovered and names, rings, magnetic field tilt, wind speeds, Miranda range, Challenger date, Saturn geometry days."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ctx5YLJfX9yL6b2WGfJsof","type":"tool_result","content":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter: Key Details\n\n**Journey & Approach**\nThe spacecraft traveled approximately 4.5 years from Saturn to reach Uranus. Long-range observations commenced on November 4, 1985, when \"signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\"\n\n**Closest Approach**\nVoyager 2 reached its nearest point on January 24, 1986, at 17:59 UT, flying within \"about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers)\" of the planet.\n\n**Observational Conditions**\nLight levels were extremely dim, with conditions \"400 times less than terrestrial conditions,\" presenting significant challenges for imaging.\n\n**Scientific Discoveries**\nThe spacecraft found \"10 new moons\" and \"two new rings in addition to the 'older' nine rings.\" It also detected \"a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"\n\n**Atmospheric Data**\nWind speeds reached \"as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour)\" in the upper atmosphere.\n\n**Moon Encounters**\nVoyager 2 passed Miranda at \"only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers),\" its closest approach to any object during its decade-long journey to that point.\n\n**Mission Context**\nThe Challenger disaster occurred the same week as the Uranus encounter announcement, on January 28, 1986."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7f4113a-c749-4d8d-8d8d-18d3200e9258","uuid":"c8461e15-8398-4dae-aece-4a001d39f9da","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:05:39.564Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":327590,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Voyager 2's Uranus Encounter: Key Details\n\n**Journey & Approach**\nThe spacecraft traveled approximately 4.5 years from Saturn to reach Uranus. Long-range observations commenced on November 4, 1985, when \"signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\"\n\n**Closest Approach**\nVoyager 2 reached its nearest point on January 24, 1986, at 17:59 UT, flying within \"about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers)\" of the planet.\n\n**Observational Conditions**\nLight levels were extremely dim, with conditions \"400 times less than terrestrial conditions,\" presenting significant challenges for imaging.\n\n**Scientific Discoveries**\nThe spacecraft found \"10 new moons\" and \"two new rings in addition to the 'older' nine rings.\" It also detected \"a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"\n\n**Atmospheric Data**\nWind speeds reached \"as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour)\" in the upper atmosphere.\n\n**Moon Encounters**\nVoyager 2 passed Miranda at \"only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers),\" its closest approach to any object during its decade-long journey to that point.\n\n**Mission Context**\nThe Challenger disaster occurred the same week as the Uranus encounter announcement, on January 28, 1986.","durationMs":6242,"url":"<redacted>
