{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfSR3usn2cv87HZpcSCWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":852,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":852},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7f4113a-c749-4d8d-8d8d-18d3200e9258","uuid":"d19b7fe7-d15d-4c7e-b7b1-a51493312440","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:05:59.539Z","request_id":"req_011CfSR3uQVU7FubQe9sYz9X"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfSR3usn2cv87HZpcSCWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical titles; base kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical title to base.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'Ur anus' typo and spacecraft name.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected name, date, range type, decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected time zone, year, units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected counts, names, tilt, author, year.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind speed units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected number format and duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected timing, crew count, date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 ... about 4,5 years\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 4.5 years; spacecraft is Voyager 2.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"Ur anus ... Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Typo; Voyager 2 is the Uranus flyby craft.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations ... began Jan. 31, 1987 ... 2,5 hours\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Nov. 4, 1985 long-range start, 2.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 400 times.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives UT, 1986, miles first.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons ... Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida ... Goethe ... 1687 ... three new rings ... eight rings ... 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page, read (counts, rings, tilt); names, Shakespeare, 1787 from own knowledge\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 10, two, nine, 55; names are standard.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour)\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 450 mph (724 km/h).\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Misspelled names of Uranian moons.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels.\",\"corrects\":\"17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers) ... nearly century-long\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 17,560 and decade-long.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"the same day ... six astronauts ... Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page, read (Jan. 28, 1986, same week); seven crew own knowledge\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Jan. 28 and same week; crew was seven.\"}]}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":852,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":852},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7f4113a-c749-4d8d-8d8d-18d3200e9258","uuid":"1839d7e9-c08c-4cb0-a44d-73256de2681f","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:06:17.240Z","request_id":"req_011CfSR3uQVU7FubQe9sYz9X"}
{"duration_api_ms":47004,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c7f4113a-c749-4d8d-8d8d-18d3200e9258","total_cost_usd":0.1329974,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13109,"cache_read_input_tokens":15027,"output_tokens":5928,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1948},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13109,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5589,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation_input_tokens":852,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":852},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":5928,"cacheReadInputTokens":15027,"cacheCreationInputTokens":13109,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.11472940000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1948,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":16623,"outputTokens":329,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.018268,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":2,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical titles; base kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical title to base.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'Ur anus' typo and spacecraft name.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected name, date, range type, decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected time zone, year, units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected counts, names, tilt, author, year.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind speed units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected number format and duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected timing, crew count, date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 ... about 4,5 years\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 4.5 years; spacecraft is Voyager 2.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 2 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"Ur anus ... Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Typo; Voyager 2 is the Uranus flyby craft.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations ... began Jan. 31, 1987 ... 2,5 hours\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Nov. 4, 1985 long-range start, 2.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 400 times.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives UT, 1986, miles first.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons ... Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida ... Goethe ... 1687 ... three new rings ... eight rings ... 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page, read (counts, rings, tilt); names, Shakespeare, 1787 from own knowledge\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 10, two, nine, 55; names are standard.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour)\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 450 mph (724 km/h).\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Misspelled names of Uranian moons.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its decade-long travels.\",\"corrects\":\"17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers) ... nearly century-long\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-2/), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives 17,560 and decade-long.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"the same day ... six astronauts ... Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page, read (Jan. 28, 1986, same week); seven crew own knowledge\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Jan. 28 and same week; crew was seven.\"}]}","ttft_ms":2848,"type":"result","duration_ms":48325,"uuid":"b32a284a-cd4e-48ef-ace9-454156b65e02","ttft_stream_ms":1617,"time_to_request_ms":105,"first_content_frame_ms":1617,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
